\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Snapshot of 14 October 2026, measured on the delivered data.}}{8}{table.caption.10}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces E2: recovering 826 hidden references of 219 papers published after 2020. Every method calls the recommendation function of the app.}}{22}{table.caption.30}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces E3 ablation on 120 reference profiles, computed by the first evaluation script, which scores methods directly. Cocitation and fusion were tested separately (Table~\ref {tab:holdoutfusion}). Higher diversity and coverage mean a more varied list and do not replace relevance.}}{22}{table.caption.33}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces E3 rerun through the recommendation function of the app, with cocitation and fusion included.}}{23}{table.caption.36}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Metrics at 10. Hit is the share of tasks with at least one answer in the top 10. The logistic regression is trained at the 2018 cutoff and is not part of the app; the E2 columns use the model trained on hidden reference tasks and the E2b columns the model trained on author tasks.}}{23}{table.caption.37}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces nDCG@10 of the decision model and of fusion on the same tasks. Hit@10 is that of the decision model.}}{25}{table.caption.41}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces nDCG@10 when the reader also describes a topic. BM25 alone ranks the same candidates by their BM25 score for the topic. Hit@10 is that of the decision model with a topic.}}{25}{table.caption.44}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Share of tasks with at least one right paper among the first 10 and 5 recommendations.}}{26}{table.caption.45}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Three design rounds for the explanations. ``Read by hand'' counts correct sentences among the first ones that passed the checker. Round 1 used 16 threads on 8 cores to read the prompt, so its times are higher than they should be.}}{26}{table.caption.48}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces nDCG@10 when a language model replaces the decision model as the ranker. The 20 candidates contain 0.362, 0.172 and 0.470 of the right papers on the three tasks, and the 100 candidates contain 0.664, 0.372 and 0.745; every ranker in the two lower groups is bounded by these shares. The app version of the decision model is not bounded and is shown for reference. SLM times were measured while the machine had a background load of 12 to 16.}}{28}{table.caption.49}%
